# acp — run-agent

REQUIRES: krunvm, WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL=1, a connected coding agent OS: macOS COST: runs against the user's own coding-agent subscription

Hand a task + a worktree to a connected coding agent (Codex / Claude Code) running in an isolated local microVM. Use for autonomous, multi-step changes you want a full external agent to make on a checkout.

app :acp__run_agent do
  title       "acp — run-agent"
  description "REQUIRES: krunvm, WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL=1, a connected coding agent OS: macOS COST: runs against the user's own coding-agent subscription Hand a task + a worktree to a connected coding agent (Codex / Claude Code) running in an isolated local microVM. — NETWORK. DESTRUCTIVE"
end

## The mental model
One command boots a throwaway microVM, mounts the worktree at `/work`, copies the agent's auth in, runs the agent against the task, and tears the VM down. The worktree edits persist on the host; nothing else is shared. The VM — not the agent's inner sandbox — is the isolation boundary.

## Workflow

### 1. Confirm a coding agent is connected (else stop + tell the user)
```bash
  # The orchestrator refuses if nothing is connected. Don't proceed without one.
  test "${WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL:-}" = "1" || { echo "acp is experimental — WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL=1 required"; exit 1; }
```

### 2. Run the agent in a microVM against a worktree
```bash
  # acp-run.sh <codex|claude> <worktree-dir> <task...>
  # the macOS-Keychain bridge), runs it, prints the transcript. Worktree edits land
  # on the host. Default sandbox is danger-full-access (the VM is the boundary).
  WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL=1 runtime/acp-local/acp-run.sh codex "$WORKTREE" "$TASK"
```

### 3. Inspect what the agent changed
```bash
  git -C "$WORKTREE" status --short
  git -C "$WORKTREE" diff
```

## Common pitfalls
- **`refusing: set WB_ACP_EXPERIMENTAL=1`** — the gate; export it.
isn't signed in on the host. Tell the user to connect/sign in (desktop Integrations) first; do not try to authenticate from inside the agent.
- **First run is slow (~1–2 min)** — the microVM installs the agent on first boot
(the bare node base). Subsequent design (prebuilt image) is faster; not a hang.
- **Don't pass secrets in the task** — the agent already has the user's auth via
the mounted copy; never echo keys into the prompt.
- **Worktree, not the whole repo** — point it at a scratch worktree, not your main
checkout, so the agent's edits are easy to review/revert.

## Verification checklist
- [ ] A coding agent was connected before invoking.
- [ ] The task + worktree were explicit; the agent ran to completion.
- [ ] `git status`/`diff` in the worktree shows the expected edits and nothing stray.

